// StrictConcurrency.xcconfig
//
// Measured settings for the staged Swift 6 concurrency migration.
// Full measurements, diagnostic categories and rationale:
//   docs/swift6-concurrency-assessment.md
//
// This file changes NOTHING until a target's build configuration is pointed at
// it (Xcode: project navigator > target > Info > Configurations > Based on
// Configuration File). It is committed unwired on purpose, so that the first
// commit of this migration touches no target settings and therefore cannot
// conflict with an in-flight branch's project.pbxproj.
//
// Measured on Apple Swift 6.4 (swiftlang-6.4.0.20.104) / Xcode 27.0 (27A5194q),
// against arm64-apple-ios18.4-simulator, Debug configuration.
//
// HARD CONSTRAINT: never set these on the `xcodebuild` command line for this
// project. A command-line setting also applies to the SwiftPM package targets,
// and SWIFT_STRICT_CONCURRENCY=complete crashes the Swift compiler while it
// builds the vendor SDK's FirebaseAuth target. These settings must stay scoped
// to this project's own targets.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stage 1 - UI test target (CatCareCalendarUITests)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Verified: ** TEST BUILD SUCCEEDED **, 1145 warnings -> 6.
// 1144 of those 1145 warnings came from one file,
// CatCareCalendarUITests/CriticalFlowsUITests.swift, because XCUIApplication is
// main-actor-isolated while XCTest methods are nonisolated.
//
// Apply to the UI test target ONLY. Do not apply to CatCareCalendarTests: that
// Swift Testing suite already produces zero warnings under `complete`, and
// forcing main-actor isolation on it would serialise a suite that currently
// runs in parallel, for no diagnostic benefit.
//
//   SWIFT_STRICT_CONCURRENCY = complete
//   SWIFT_DEFAULT_ACTOR_ISOLATION = MainActor
//
// 5 override-isolation warnings remain after this, all in CriticalFlowsUITests:
// init(), init(invocation:), init(selector:), setUpWithError(),
// tearDownWithError(). Each needs its isolation reconciled with the nonisolated
// XCTestCase declaration it overrides.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stage 3 - app target, warning mode
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Measured: 19 warnings in 14 files, full coverage (163 of 167 app files), and
// 0 errors ONCE the toolchain bug at Views/NotificationSettingsView.swift:64
// is worked around.
//
// CORRECTION (verified by building it): line 64 is the trailing
// .settingsCardRowStyle() of `statusSection`, NOT the `notificationTogglesSection`
// declaration three lines below it. Extracting notificationTogglesSection alone
// does not clear the error - it only moves the reported line. `statusSection` is
// the expression that has to move into its own View struct. Both sections were
// extracted in the end (NotificationPermissionSection and
// NotificationTogglesSection), and the app target then built with 0 errors.
//
// Re-measured after that extraction on the same toolchain: 19 warnings in 13
// files, 169 of 169 app files, identical across two clean builds. The file count
// is 13 rather than 14 because the Views/Cat/EnhancedCatCardView.swift
// `import SwiftData` defect was fixed at the same time.
//
// Until the extraction lands, this setting FAILS the build on that one compiler
// defect ("failed to produce diagnostic for expression").
//
//   SWIFT_STRICT_CONCURRENCY = complete
//
// SWIFT_STRICT_CONCURRENCY = targeted is deliberately not offered: it was
// measured and produces the same single warning as the baseline. It buys
// nothing here.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stage 5 - app target, default actor isolation (DECIDE ON EVIDENCE, NOT NOW)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Measured cheaper up front than plain `complete`: 15 warnings in 9 files
// against 19 in 14, at equal full coverage. It removes the static-singleton and
// main-actor-UIKit categories outright.
//
// It is not a free win. It moves the cost into the SwiftData @Model types -
// 9 warnings across Models/Cat.swift, Models/CareTask.swift and
// Models/TaskAssistantViewModel.swift - which is the part of this local-first
// codebase that most deserves care. Re-measure before choosing.
//
//   SWIFT_DEFAULT_ACTOR_ISOLATION = MainActor
//   SWIFT_UPCOMING_FEATURE_NONISOLATED_NONSENDING_BY_DEFAULT = YES
//
// RE-MEASURED 2026-09-09, against this branch's finished state rather than the
// pre-stage-4 baseline. Isolation default only; the NonisolatedNonsending flag
// was NOT set, so this is not comparable with the 15/9 figure above.
//   Baseline, `complete` alone:            0 warnings from repository files.
//   With SWIFT_DEFAULT_ACTOR_ISOLATION:   21 warnings in 9 files.
// So the setting adds 21 warnings to a clean branch. 8 of the 21 sit in the two
// files that hold all five @Model types (Models/Cat.swift, Models/CareTask.swift).
// Recommendation on this evidence: do not adopt it. It relocates the cost into
// the SwiftData models, which is the least safe place in a local-first app to
// take on new isolation debt. Full breakdown: the stage 5 section of
// docs/swift6-concurrency-assessment.md.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stage 6 - language mode, LAST
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Flip this only once stage 3 reports 0 warnings. NOT MET as of 2026-09-09: a clean
// build reports 0 warnings from repository files but 2 `#Predicate` macro-expansion warnings, both
// "this is an error in the Swift 6 language mode". See the stage 6 section of
// docs/swift6-concurrency-assessment.md. Count macro diagnostics separately from path-scoped ones. It does NOT raise the minimum
// iOS version: 10 builds against iOS 18.4 produced zero availability
// diagnostics in any stage. iOS 18 remains the minimum.
//
//   SWIFT_VERSION = 6.0
//
// Keep these three Swift 6.2-era APIs out of the app target while iOS 18 is the
// minimum - each was probed and each carries an iOS 26.0 floor:
//   Task.immediate, InlineArray, withTaskPriorityEscalationHandler
// These were probed and ARE available on iOS 18.4:
//   Synchronization.Mutex, Synchronization.Atomic, isolated deinit, Task(name:)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Measurement aid, not a shipping setting
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Intended to raise how far a build gets before the module stops. Measured: it
// helps a WARNING-mode build (it took the main-actor warning stage from 143 to
// the full 163 of 167 files) but it does NOT help error mode - Swift 6 mode fell
// from 142 files to 103 with the flag. Error mode never reached more than 142,
// against 163 in warning mode, and its coverage was not reproducible between
// runs. That gap is the reason this migration measures in warning mode and flips
// the language mode once, at the end.
//
//   OTHER_SWIFT_FLAGS = $(inherited) -continue-building-after-errors
